COMMENT     On Oct 21, 2003 this sequence version replaced gi:21930298.
            The transposon Tn21 and many Tn21-like transposons carry within
            them a potentially mobile DNA element called the integron which
            encodes a RecA-independent, site-specific integration system
            responsible for the acquisition of multiple antibiotic resistance
            genes. Tn21 also carries the genes involved in its own
            transposition (tnp) and a mercury resistance (mer) operon.
            Recently the last section of the Tn21 transposon was sequenced and
            the entire sequence (19,671 bp) is available in five overlapping
            GenBank sequences (GenBank: X04891, M10791, X12870, U42226, and
            J01730). We have assembled these sequences and updated the
            annotation of the assembled sequence. Due to the recent interest in
            integrons, we elected to present the Tn21 sequence with the
            transposition genes on the left and the mer genes on the right
            since this allows viewing the majority of the integron components
            in the orientation favored by those studying the integron itself.
            Data entry by Alice L. Watson.
